# policies/redis.policy
#
# Written from observing `redis-server`'s real behavior first
# (strace -f), not guessed:
#
#   - socket(), bind(), listen() each happen exactly ONCE, from the
#     main thread, before any clients connect.
#   - Redis then clone3()s 3-4 background threads (CLONE_THREAD —
#     i.e. real POSIX threads, same thread-group ID as the main
#     thread). supervisor.c's get_tgid() maps every notification
#     from these threads back to the SAME tracked proc_state as the
#     main thread automatically — no extra policy work needed, and
#     none of these threads call any of our trapped syscalls in
#     normal operation.
#   - accept4() — NOT accept() — is the call Redis uses, and only
#     fires when a client actually connects; an idle Redis makes NO
#     accept4() calls at all (it blocks in epoll_wait, which we
#     don't trap), so there is no idle-polling overhead from this
#     supervisor.
#   - No connect(), execve(), execveat(), or sendto() were observed
#     in normal operation (we run it with --save "" --appendonly no
#     so no background-save fork happens either).
#
# Run (binary path may differ — `which redis-server`):
#   ./supervisor --policy policies/redis.policy \
#       /usr/bin/redis-server --port 6380 --bind 127.0.0.1 \
#       --save "" --appendonly no --daemonize no
#
# In another terminal: redis-cli -p 6380 ping / set / get, or
# redis-benchmark -p 6380 ...

state INIT
state BOUND
state LISTENING

INIT      socket    allow
INIT      bind      allow -> BOUND
INIT      connect   allow
INIT      execve    allow -> INIT
INIT      execveat  allow -> INIT

BOUND     socket    allow
BOUND     bind      allow
BOUND     listen    allow -> LISTENING
BOUND     connect   allow
BOUND     execve    allow -> INIT
BOUND     execveat  allow -> INIT

LISTENING socket    allow
LISTENING bind      allow
LISTENING listen    allow
LISTENING accept    allow
LISTENING accept4   allow
LISTENING connect   deny
LISTENING execve    deny
LISTENING execveat  deny
LISTENING sendto    deny
